.TH SAPHIRA-IMAGE 8 "2026-09-26" "Saphira Linux" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
saphira\-image \- build a bootable Saphira disk image
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B saphira\-image
.B build
.B \-\-target
.I TARGET
.B \-\-output
.I IMAGE
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B saphira\-image
.B list\-targets
.br
.B saphira\-image
.B plan
.B \-\-target
.I TARGET
.RI [ \-\-size
.IR SIZE ]
.br
.B saphira\-image
.B report
.B \-\-target
.I TARGET
.RI [ \-\-role
.IR ROLE ]
...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B saphira\-image
partitions a disk image, installs Saphira into it through the canonical
installer, prepares the requested init system, installs a bootloader, and
reports what went in.
.PP
It supports four targets, covering both init systems and both boot
methods:
.PP
.RS
.TS
l l l.
bios\-openrc     BIOS,   OpenRC
bios\-systemd    BIOS,   systemd
uefi\-openrc     UEFI,   OpenRC
uefi\-systemd    UEFI,   systemd
.TE
.RE
.PP
A target is a row in
.IR files/targets ,
not an implementation. Assembly is common; only the partition plan, the
bootloader invocation and the init preparation differ.
.SH USAGE
The tool is a one-shot administrative command and never runs as a service.
It needs root, because it creates loop devices and mounts filesystems, and
it must run on a host that has the partitioning and formatting tools
available: util-linux for sfdisk and losetup, e2fsprogs for mkfs.ext4,
dosfstools for mkfs.vfat, and grub for grub-install. qemu is needed only for
a .qcow2 output.
.PP
.BR saphira\-image " plan" ", " list\-targets " and " report
are static: they need neither root nor the assembler, so a partition plan
can be inspected on a machine that could not build an image.
.SH ARCHITECTURE
Ownership is split deliberately, so that there is exactly one assembler
and exactly one source of dependency truth:
.TP
.B saphira\-image
the target matrix, the partition plan, the bootloader invocation, init
preparation, and measurement.
.TP
.BR saphira\-install (8)
the mounted\-target installation.
.TP
.BR saphira\-assemble\-rootfs (8)
rootfs assembly and the
.B accounts.d
/
.B sysusers.d
identity replay.
.TP
APK metadata
the dependency closure. This tool never enumerates a resolved closure.
.PP
This tool never unpacks an APK, never writes a package list of its own,
and will not substitute its own installer for
.BR saphira\-install .
.SH INIT SELECTION
The init system is an explicit operator choice carried by the target. It
is never detected.
.PP
Packages ship BOTH init definitions unconditionally; unit files are inert
where systemd is not installed and OpenRC scripts are inert where it is.
What differs between an
.B \-openrc
and a
.B \-systemd
image is which set is
.IR enabled ,
and whether the boot path is consistent with what is on disk. Nothing
here infers an active init from installed files, and nothing teaches APK
installation to guess.
.PP
For an OpenRC target,
.B /etc/inittab
is checked to select
.BR /sbin/openrc ,
and enabled services are written as
.I /etc/runlevels/default
symlinks rather than by invoking
.BR rc\-update ,
which expects a running init.
.PP
For a systemd target,
.B default.target
points at
.BR multi\-user.target ,
and services are enabled offline with
.BR "systemctl \-\-root=/ enable" .
Nothing is ever started: an image has no running init, and enabling is a
persistent intent.
.SH PARTITION PLANS
The two plans are genuinely different and neither is derived from the
other by arithmetic.
.TP
.B bios
GRUB's
.I core.img
must land somewhere the BIOS can find at boot. On a GPT layout that
means a dedicated
.B bios\-boot
partition, not the post\-MBR gap a DOS layout would use. There is no ESP:
a BIOS system has no firmware to read one.
.TP
.B uefi
The firmware reads a FAT EFI System Partition. BIOS partitioning rules
are deliberately not reused: a
.B bios\-boot
partition means nothing to UEFI firmware, and without a real ESP the
firmware finds no bootloader at all. The ESP is formatted FAT32, mounted at
.IR /boot/efi ,
and
.B grub\-install
writes its payload there.
.PP
The first partition begins at sector 2048 for 1 MiB alignment, and
.B saphira\-image
refuses to emit a plan whose last sector exceeds the requested image
size. The plan is generated, never hand-written per target, and
.B saphira\-image plan
prints it for inspection without building anything.
.SH PROFILES AND ROLES
A profile lists
.I intentions
only. Dependency closure belongs to APK metadata and is never duplicated
into an image definition.
.PP
The minimal profiles carry exactly what a bootable, manageable machine
needs, and each entry is justified in the profile file itself. Role
layers add intentions on top. A role is profile data, not a meta\-package:
no new package name is invented for a role, and the package manager still
owns the closure.
.SH MEASUREMENT
.B saphira\-image report
and the end of every build report the package count, rootfs size, image
size and the largest packages in the payload. These figures are reported
for visibility, not as a target. No functionality is removed to improve
them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-target " NAME"
One of the four targets.
.TP
.BI \-\-output " PATH"
Image path. A
.B .raw
suffix produces a raw image; a
.B .qcow2
suffix produces a compressed one and requires
.BR qemu\-img .
An existing path is refused rather than overwritten.
.TP
.BI \-\-size " SIZE"
Minimum image size, accepting
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
.BR G ,
.BR T .
Defaults to 2G.
.TP
.BI \-\-repository " PATH"
Repository index to install from. Defaults to the host repository.
.TP
.BI \-\-hostname " NAME"
Hostname written into the image. Defaults to
.BR saphira .
.TP
.BI \-\-root\-password " PW"
Root password set inside the image.
.TP
.BI \-\-role " NAME"
Add a role layer. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-role\-file " PATH"
Add intentions from a file. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-enable
Install the profile but enable none of its services.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-work
Retain the scratch workspace when a build fails, for diagnosis.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero on success. Non\-zero on any refusal. A failed build removes the
partial image it created; a pre\-existing output path is never touched.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SAPHIRA_TMPDIR
Scratch location. Defaults to
.BR /build/tmp .
Saphira tool scratch never uses host
.BR /tmp .
.TP
.B SAPHIRA_INSTALL
Override the
.B saphira\-install
path.
.TP
.B SAPHIRA_CHROOT
Override the
.B saphira\-chroot
path.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/saphira-image
the tool.
.TP
.I /usr/share/saphira/image/targets
the target matrix: one row per supported boot target, each naming a boot
method, an init system and a profile. This is data, so adding a target is a
line rather than a new implementation.
.TP
.I /usr/share/saphira/image/profiles/
minimal profiles and role layers. A profile lists
.I intentions
only; dependency closure belongs to APK metadata.
.TP
.I /usr/share/saphira/image/services.d/
OPTIONAL package-to-service mapping, one file per package, one init
service or unit base name per line. A package normally names its service
after itself, so an absent directory means every service is called after
its package. The exception is real:
.BR saphira\-packager " ships " saphira\-build ,
not
.BR saphira\-packager ,
so assuming the two names match enabled nothing at all, silently. The
mapping is data beside the profiles rather than something a profile
names, because a profile lists package
.I intentions
only
.RB ( saphira-profiles (5));
enumerating a package's services in a profile would duplicate package
knowledge in the image definition and rot whenever the package changes.
.TP
.I /etc/saphira/
controller configuration, owned by saphira-packager.
.SH NOTES
Neither
.B saphira\-install
nor
.B saphira\-assemble\-rootfs
is modified by this package, and neither is duplicated here. If the
canonical path cannot express something the image needs, the correct change
is to those tools and not a second assembler here.
.PP
Packages continue to ship BOTH init definitions. The image creator chooses
which set is enabled; it does not change what a package ships, and no
package is rebuilt for a different init.
.PP
The reported sizes are measurements, not targets. Removing functionality to
improve them would be the wrong trade for a server image.
.SH EXAMPLES
Inspect a partition plan without building anything:
.PP
.RS
.nf
saphira\-image plan \-\-target uefi\-systemd \-\-size 8G
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build a minimal UEFI/systemd server image:
.PP
.RS
.nf
saphira\-image build \-\-target uefi\-systemd \-\-size 8G \\
    \-\-output saphira-server.qcow2 \-\-hostname srv1 \\
    \-\-role role\-networking
.fi
.RE
.SH AUTHOR
AKADATA LIMITED, for Saphira Linux.
